<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marinid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marinid, Marinids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marinid dynasty was a Berber dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> from the mid-thirteenth to the fifteenth century. The Marinids rose as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> disintegrated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> in 1269, ending Almohad rule. They inherited administrative and commercial structures from the Almohad and Almoravid periods and were noted patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Marinid dynasty" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Askelaadden (minor modifications by R Prazeres) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marinids_Zayyanids_and_Hafsids_c1360.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Marinids were a faction of the Zenata Berber tribal confederation. The Banu Marin were nomads from the Zab, a region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biskra">Biskra</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, who were pushed out after Arab Bedouins arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. They entered what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in force by the beginning of the 13th century. They first submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>. Their leader Mahyu contributed to the Almohad victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a> in 1195, though he died of his wounds, and his son Abd al-Haqq was the effective founder of the dynasty.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> on 16 July 1212, the state was weakened. From 1213 or 1214 the Marinids began to tax farming communities in the north-east of today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and from 1215 fighting with the Almohads broke out regularly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early rulers governed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, first at Taza, then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> from 1244. Abu Yusuf Ya'qub captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> in 1269, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> rule, and the later rulers were sultans of Fez and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. The Marinids supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and after Granada ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> they tried to control the traffic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a>. By 1294 they had occupied Rota, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>. In the mid-14th century, under Abu al-Hasan and his son Abu Inan, they briefly held sway over most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, including large parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. They were defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado">Battle of Río Salado</a> in 1340, and the Castilian capture of Algeciras in 1344 expelled them for good from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris">Abu Inan Faris</a> in 1358, real power lay with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> while sultans succeeded one another quickly. In 1359 Hintata tribesmen from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> and governed it independently until 1526. From the early 15th century the related Wattasid dynasty competed with the Marinids, whose last sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-haqq-ii">Abd al-Haqq II</a>, reigned until 1465.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Marinids largely continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> structures, ruling a tribal state with little civil administration beyond the capital and relying on family members as governors. Their army was drawn from loyal tribes, other Zenata tribes, Arab tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> and Banu Ma'qil, and Christian mercenaries who served as the sultan's bodyguard. They made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> their capital and restored Maliki Sunni Islam as the official religion. The Marinids built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> across the country, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">Qarawiyyin</a> of Fez reached its height of prestige. They were the first to use zellij tilework extensively.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marinid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Almohad unification of the Maghreb, the only time it was united under a single Berber dynasty, created an administrative and commercial framework inherited by the Hafsid, Zayyanid and <b>Marinid</b> successor states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty brought together Saharan Berber, Moroccan and Andalusian traditions in architecture and art, and the Almohad and <b>Marinid</b> dynasties built on their work.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Alfonso XI won the decisive Battle of Río Salado in 1340 over the <b>Marinids</b> of Morocco and Yusuf I of Granada and took Algeciras in 1344, ending large North African interventions in the peninsula, though he died of plague while besieging Gibraltar in 1350.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1275 the <b>Marinid</b> sultans of Morocco sent armies across the Strait, and a corps of North African volunteers, the Ghuzat al-Jihad under their own commander, served Granada for most of the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>For a time several Andalusi cities and the young <b>Marinid</b> state also offered him nominal allegiance, making Tunis the strongest Muslim capital in the western Mediterranean when he died in 1249.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the 14th century local writers portrayed the Idrisids as the starting point of an Islamic Maghrib al-Aqsa, the Furthest West; Ibn Abi Zar's Rawd al-Qirtas promoted continuity between Idrisid and Marinid rule, and both the <b>Marinids</b> and the Wattasids sought at times to associate themselves with the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
